<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mozhaysk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mozhaysk is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the upper Moskva river about 110 km west of Moscow. Taken by Moscow from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1303, it was the centre of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> principality until 1454 and a fortress guarding the western approach to the capital. The battlefield of Borodino, scene of the great battle of 1812, lies nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozhaysk.jpg" alt="Mozhaysk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kishjar? · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mozhaysk-saint-nicholas-church-july-2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mozhaysk lies 110 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on the historic road leading to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It is the administrative centre of Mozhaysky District. One theory holds that its name comes from the Mozhay river, whose name may be of Baltic origin, compared with the Lithuanian mažoji, small, in contrast to the larger Moskva river nearby. The town was frequently the last major obstacle on the way to the capital, and gave rise to the Russian expression to push beyond Mozhay, meaning to crush an enemy completely and drive them far away. It is said to have originated when the Polish army retreated to Mozhaysk after the Battle of Moscow in 1612.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mozhaysk is first mentioned in 1231 as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>. It became an important stronghold of the Smolensk dynasty and in the 13th century was ruled by Duke Theodore the Black, later a saint. The Muscovites seized it in 1303, but during the following century they had serious trouble defending it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1345 to 1377. A younger brother of the ruling Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> usually held the Principality of Mozhaysk. In 1562 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> signed the Treaty of Mozhaysk there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, and in 1708 the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> granted the town its status.</p><p>The town helped defend the western approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in the 19th and 20th centuries. During the French invasion of 1812 the Battle of Borodino was fought 12 kilometres away. German forces took Mozhaysk on 18 October 1941 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recaptured it on 20 January 1942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A first fortified settlement existed in the 12th century. After the great fire of 1541 Ivan the Terrible ordered it rebuilt, and a brick kremlin replaced the stone fortress in 1624 to 1626. The Cathedral of St. Nicholas, in Gothic Revival style by Aleksei Bakarev, was begun in 1802, ransacked by the retreating French in 1812 and consecrated in 1814. The Luzhetsky Monastery was founded in 1408 by Saint Ferapont and rebuilt in brick in the 16th century. The 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 31,363 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mozhaysk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mozhaysk</b>, on the Moskva river west of Moscow, was taken from Smolensk in 1303.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mozhaysk">Principality of Mozhaysk</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike his brother Ivan of Mozhaysk, Mikhail stayed loyal to Vasily II during Moscow's civil war, and he was allowed to keep his lands after <b>Mozhaysk</b> was taken in 1454.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-vereya">Principality of Vereya</a></li></ul></section>
